    "case_id": "692",
    "case_summary": "A 33-year-old female presented with mediastinal mass. The patient had no discomfort within 5 days after FNA. On day 6, high fever and chest pain occurred. On day 7, the patient had dyspnea with low breath sound in left lung. During MRI, the patient had sudden convulsions and loss of consciousness.\n\nBlood pressure 109/77 mmHg, heart rate 92 bpm, respiratory rate 20 breaths/min, body temperature 37.9°C, clear respiratory sounds in both lungs without rales. Later examination showed low breath sound in left lung.\n\n- Laboratory findings: White cell count 12,110/μL (initially), later increased to 31,400/μL; Hemoglobin 14.6 g/dL; C-reactive protein 20.85 mg/L, later increased to 261.62 mg/L; PO2 82 mmHg, later decreased to 59 mmHg\n  - Imaging Studies:\n    CT findings: Mass of soft tissue density in mediastinum adjacent to esophagus, measuring 4.2cm×3.5cm, CT value 50HU\n    EUS findings: Hypoechoic lesion of 40mm outside the esophagus, close to thoracic aorta, uniform internal echo without obvious blood flow signal\n    MRI findings: Enlarged mediastinal mass with signal intensity similar to muscles\n    Chest radiograph: Complete atelectasis of left lung\n  - Bacterial Culture: Cyst fluid positive for Streptococcus pyogenes, sensitive to penicillin and vancomycin",
